/// \defgroup grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection W3D: DX8Caps adapter and driver capability detection
/// \ingroup grp_w3d_rendering
/// \brief How WW3D2 finds out what the video card and its driver can do:
///        vendor and device classification, per-format support tables,
///        shader and texture limits, driver-version verdicts and the
///        workarounds applied to specific hardware.
///
/// DX8Caps is a plain record of capabilities. A constructor fills it once,
/// from what Direct3D reports about an adapter, and the rest of the
/// renderer then asks it questions through DX8Wrapper::Get_Current_Caps
/// (\ref sub_w3d_rendering). This group documents the code that fills it in:
/// the vendor and device lookups, the format probes and the rule tables.
/// The names are Westwood's, from Zero Hour's dx8caps unit; the probe and
/// rule behaviour below is read from the BFME 2 bodies, and a statement that
/// comes only from Zero Hour's source is marked "Zero Hour". Which of these
/// functions the project has recovered, named or byte-matched at any moment
/// is not tracked here; use the project's progress tools
/// (reverse/functions.csv searched with rg, tools/callers_of.py and
/// tools/next_work.py).
///
/// The vendor and device lookups, the probes and the rule functions are
/// private members of DX8Caps. They are described in the sections of this
/// page: \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_lookups,
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_probes,
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_driver_table and
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules. The two public entry
/// points, DX8Caps::Compute_Caps and DX8Caps::Is_Valid_Display_Format, have
/// their own entries at the end of the page.
///
/// \par The retail object differs from Zero Hour's DX8Caps
/// BFME 2's object has the same name and the same job as Zero Hour's class
/// but is built on Direct3D 9 data. The differences that matter to a reader:
/// - The adapter identifier that DX8Caps::Compute_Caps reads has Direct3D 9's
///   layout, which adds a device-name string before the driver version.
/// - The capability record is the Direct3D 9 caps structure, which is larger
///   than the Direct3D 8 one.
/// - The object keeps the raw PCI device identifier beside the mapped device
///   index, and has a dynamic-texture flag.
/// - The Z-bias flag tests Direct3D 9's depth-bias raster capability bit, and
///   the dynamic-texture flag tests a Direct3D 9 only bit of the second caps
///   word.
/// - The depth-stencil probe asks for the Direct3D 9 format codes by number,
///   and its switch names two codes (the float depth formats) that exist only
///   in Direct3D 9.
/// - The three format tables are longer and are indexed by the Direct3D
///   format code itself (see \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_texture_format).
///
/// \par When the object is built
/// - DX8Wrapper::Enumerate_Devices builds a temporary DX8Caps for each
///   adapter that reports at least pixel shader 1.1, using the caps-copy
///   constructor and an unknown display format. With an unknown display
///   format the three format tables are cleared and nothing is probed. The
///   enumeration uses this object only to call
///   DX8Caps::Is_Valid_Display_Format for every display mode of five
///   formats. Enumerate_Devices leaves a mode that fails that test out of
///   the device's resolution list, and a device with no valid mode is left
///   out of the device list. The ceilings set by
///   \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules therefore decide which
///   modes that list keeps.
///   Zero Hour: the options menu lists that set of modes through the
///   getDisplayModeCount method of W3DDisplay, which applies further filters
///   of its own. BFME 2's consumer of the list was not verified.
/// - DX8Wrapper::Do_Onetime_Device_Dependent_Inits begins by deleting the
///   wrapper's current caps and building a new object with the device
///   constructor. The device constructor reads the caps record from the
///   wrapper's device, derives the hardware transform-and-lighting flag and
///   calls DX8Caps::Compute_Caps with the wrapper's recorded display format
///   and the current adapter identifier. The caps-copy constructor does the
///   same with a caps record the caller already holds.
/// - DX8Wrapper::Do_Onetime_Device_Dependent_Shutdowns deletes the object.
///
/// \par Who reads it
/// The readers found among the BFME 2 sources checked for this page include
/// (the list is not exhaustive; tools/callers_of.py and rg show the rest):
/// - ShaderClass::Apply (fog permission, the modulate-alpha-add-colour
///   fallback, the texture-per-pass limit, and a special case keyed on the
///   3dfx vendor and Voodoo3 device);
/// - Render2DClass::Render (the texture-per-pass limit and the dot-product
///   flag);
/// - MaterialPassClass::Install_Materials (the texture-per-pass limit);
/// - VertexMaterialClass::Apply (the vendor and the raw PCI device
///   identifier: on ATI parts whose raw identifier is one particular value or
///   falls in one short range, when exactly one of the first two texture
///   stages has a mapper it gives the unmapped stage an identity texture
///   transform; the source calls this an ATI workaround. Open-BFME-1's
///   vertmaterial source has the same rule and Zero Hour's does not);
/// - DX8IndexBufferClass::DX8IndexBufferClass (the transform-and-lighting
///   flag, which adds a software-processing usage flag when absent);
/// - DX8Wrapper::Create_Render_Target (the render-to-texture table, plus the
///   caps record's maximum texture size);
/// - DX8Wrapper::Set_Gamma (the gamma flag);
/// - Vertex_Split_Table::Vertex_Split_Table and
///   DX8FVFCategoryContainer::Generate_Texture_Categories (the N-patch flag);
/// - W3DSnowManager::ReAcquireResources (the point-sprite flag);
/// - W3DRadar::initializeTextureFormats (the texture-format table);
/// - Load_Texture(ChunkLoadClass&), the free function in TextureAssetGet.cpp
///   that MeshModelClass::read_textures calls repeatedly while it reads a W3D
///   mesh's texture chunks (the bump-envmap flag and the texture-format table:
///   when the texture is typed as a bump map and the flag is set, it forces one
///   mip level and picks the first supported of U8V8, X8L8V8U8 and L6V5U5);
/// - RenderableStandingWaterArea::createTexture (the texture-format table:
///   for its first texture it probes the same three bump formats in the same
///   order, without testing the bump flag).
/// Zero Hour: texture.cpp (Load_Texture) is the counterpart of the bump-map
/// case above and picks the formats in the same order. textureloader.cpp
/// (Is_Format_Compressed) and ww3dformat.cpp (Get_Valid_Texture_Format) read
/// the compression flag. Get_Valid_Texture_Format maps the DXT formats to
/// uncompressed ones when the flag is off or compression is not allowed. When
/// the flag is on, it falls back to an uncompressed format if the
/// texture-format table says a DXT2 to DXT5 request is missing, and it moves a
/// DXT1 request to DXT2 when DXT1 is missing and DXT2 is present (its comment
/// ties that to NVIDIA hardware). Nothing in Zero Hour's code outside the class
/// calls the log accessors or the driver-verdict accessor. Whether BFME 2 has
/// equivalent readers was not checked here.
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// Established by comparing the BFME 2 bodies with Zero Hour's source:
/// - Define_Vendor, the eight device lookups, Is_Valid_Display_Format,
///   Check_Bumpmap_Support, Check_Shader_Support,
///   Check_Maximum_Texture_Support and Check_Driver_Version_Status do what
///   Zero Hour's do, case for case.
/// - DX8Caps::Init_Caps queries the caps once. Zero Hour's switches the
///   device to software vertex processing, reads the caps, and, if the card
///   has hardware transform and lighting, switches back and reads them
///   again.
/// - The three format probes work directly on Direct3D format codes and
///   write no per-format log lines. Zero Hour converts its small WW3DFormat
///   enumeration to a Direct3D format first and logs each supported format.
///   The depth probe uses a fixed list of seven formats and asks for a
///   surface; Zero Hour asks for a texture.
/// - DX8Caps::Compute_Caps keeps the raw PCI device identifier next to the
///   mapped device index, and adds a dynamic-texture flag with a driver
///   exclusion for NVIDIA.
/// - DX8Caps::Vendor_Specific_Hacks has no resolution ceiling for the
///   GeForce2 MX cards. Zero Hour's rule names two enumerators, the GeForce2
///   MX and the GeForce2 MX 400. Get_NVidia_Device never returns the plain
///   GeForce2 MX enumerator, so in practice Zero Hour's ceiling of 1024 by
///   768 applies to the GeForce2 MX 400 only. Open-BFME-1's source has no
///   such rule either.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - None of this is data-driven. No function in this group reads an INI
///   file, a registry key or any other file: every table, every vendor rule
///   and every threshold is code. The inputs are what Direct3D reports about
///   the adapter and the driver, so a mod cannot override a rule, and the
///   only way to change one is to change the program.
/// - The flags still decide which code paths take effect. Whether
///   multitexturing, the dot-product blend, N-patches or point sprites are
///   used is decided by the flags that the readers test (see Who reads it).
///   Zero Hour: snow uses point sprites only when the weather setting's
///   SnowPointSprites field (an INI field in Zero Hour's weather data) and
///   the hardware flag both allow it. BFME 2's reader tests the same two
///   conditions; whether its field table uses the same field name was not
///   checked here.
/// - Which textures are requested as bump maps is a property of the model
///   file's texture chunk (a texture type attribute), not of this group; this
///   group only decides whether the hardware flag lets the request through.
/// - Nothing in this group touches game-logic state. The functions run on the
///   render side and the readers listed are drawing code; no connection to
///   the logic CRC or to lockstep is claimed here. Hardware flags differ from
///   machine to machine, so a reader on the logic side would break lockstep;
///   the readers listed above are all drawing code.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The seam is the set of public query functions (the Support_ and Get_
///   accessors), the format tables behind them and the cached caps record
///   that a few readers use directly. A port to another graphics API can
///   keep those and replace DX8Caps::Compute_Caps and its helpers with the
///   new API's adapter and feature queries. The adapter identifier, the
///   caps record layout and the format-code arithmetic described above are
///   the Direct3D 9 specific parts.
/// - Format codes leak out of this class. WW3DFormat values are Direct3D
///   format codes in BFME 2, and some readers pass literal codes (for
///   example W3DRadar::initializeTextureFormats, and the bump-format probes
///   in Load_Texture and RenderableStandingWaterArea::createTexture). A
///   different API needs a mapping at every such site, not only inside
///   DX8Caps.
/// - The hardware lists stop at 2002 and 2003 era parts. An adapter whose
///   PCI vendor identifier is NVIDIA's, ATI's (the identifier AMD graphics
///   cards also report) or Intel's is classified as that vendor but gets an
///   unknown device, because the device tables end at the GeForce4, the
///   Radeon 8500 and the i815. Rules that test only the vendor still apply
///   to every device of that vendor. On NVIDIA hardware the N-patch flag and
///   the first DXT format entry are forced off, and the dynamic-texture flag
///   is cleared when the fourth component of the driver version is below
///   6176. On PowerVR hardware the resolution is capped and fog is
///   disallowed. Rules keyed on a driver DLL name or on build numbers apply
///   only to the DLL names and builds listed in
///   \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_driver_table; a driver under any other
///   name keeps the unknown verdict. A port should treat the build-number
///   tests as data about 2002 era drivers, not as a check that works for
///   current ones.
/// - The rules in \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules are
///   workarounds for the hardware and drivers of the period, by the source's
///   own comments. A port can drop them, but must then check that nothing
///   downstream relied on the limits they set (the texture-per-pass limit of
///   1 on a few cards, the fog flag and the resolution ceilings).
/// - Vendor and device rules also live outside DX8Caps. VertexMaterialClass::Apply
///   tests the vendor and the raw device identifier itself, and a port must
///   find and replace every such site rather than only the rules listed here.
///   Zero Hour's W3DShaderManager::getChipset classifies the card a second time
///   in the device layer, first honouring a ChipsetType override from the
///   global data (an INI field in Zero Hour's global data table), then
///   reading the adapter's vendor and device identifiers, and failing that
///   using this class's simultaneous-texture count and pixel shader version.
///   Whether BFME 2 has the same override was not checked here.
///
/// \section grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_lookups Vendor and device lookups
/// DX8Caps::Compute_Caps calls DX8Caps::Define_Vendor first and then one
/// device lookup chosen by the result. All nine are private static members;
/// each is a plain switch over PCI identifiers, and each device lookup returns
/// an enumerator of its own vendor's device enumeration, which indexes that
/// vendor's name table. The mapped device index is only meaningful together
/// with the vendor.
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_define_vendor Define_Vendor
/// <tt>static VendorIdType DX8Caps::Define_Vendor(unsigned vendor_id)</tt>
/// maps a PCI vendor identifier from the adapter record to the vendor
/// enumeration. Everything vendor specific later in the unit keys off its
/// result: the choice of device table, the driver verdict and the workaround
/// rules. Some vendors have more than one identifier:
/// - NVIDIA: the vendor's own identifier, plus two others that the source
///   labels as STB (NVIDIA's Riva 128) and Guillemot's NVIDIA based cards.
/// - ATI, Intel, S3, PowerVR and Matrox: one identifier each.
/// - 3dfx: its own identifier, plus two that the source labels as
///   Alliance based and Macronix based reference cards.
/// - 3Dlabs: its own identifier, plus the Texas Instruments one (the
///   standard PCI assignment for TI, which presumably explains the TI P1 and
///   second Permedia2 entries that Get_3DLabs_Device lists).
///
/// Anything else returns the unknown vendor. The enumeration also has
/// Cirrus Logic and Rendition entries, and the vendor name table has names
/// for them, but this function never returns them and Compute_Caps has no
/// device lookup for them. If the result is the unknown vendor and the driver
/// file name begins with the character 3, Compute_Caps sets the vendor to
/// 3dfx instead. Provenance: Zero Hour's body case for case (compared); the
/// vendor grouping comments are the source's own.
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_get_nvidia_device Get_NVidia_Device
/// <tt>static DeviceTypeNVidia DX8Caps::Get_NVidia_Device(unsigned device_id)</tt>
/// maps an NVIDIA PCI device identifier to a device enumerator, from the RIVA
/// 128 up to the GeForce4 generation. Compute_Caps calls it when the vendor is
/// NVIDIA, logs the device's name and stores the index. In BFME 2 the result
/// is not used by any rule in Vendor_Specific_Hacks (its NVIDIA rules key on
/// the vendor only). A switch over forty-four identifiers:
/// - RIVA 128 (two identifiers, labelled STB cards in the source), NV1, TNT,
///   TNT2 and its Aladdin, Ultra, Vanta and M64 variants.
/// - GeForce 256 and DDR, Quadro, the GeForce2 family (GTS, Ultra, Ti, Go,
///   integrated, MX 100/200 and MX 400) and Quadro2 PRO and MXR.
/// - GeForce3 (plain, Ti 200, Ti 500), Quadro DCC, and the GeForce4 Ti, MX
///   and Go parts.
/// - Six identifiers of Guillemot's NVIDIA based Maxi Gamer boards, mapped
///   to the corresponding TNT, TNT2 or GeForce 256 enumerators.
///
/// Anything else returns the unknown NVIDIA device. Nothing newer than the
/// GeForce4 generation is listed, so current NVIDIA hardware always gets the
/// unknown device. The GeForce2 MX enumerator itself is never returned: the
/// two GeForce2 MX identifiers map to the MX 100/200 and MX 400 enumerators.
///
/// \warning Zero Hour: the NVIDIA name table in its source is missing a comma
/// after the GeForce2 MX entry, so that entry and the next are one string and
/// the table is one entry short of the enumeration. From the GeForce4 Ti 4600
/// enumerator onward each device is logged under the next enumerator's name,
/// and the last enumerator (Quadro DCC) indexes past the end of the array. This
/// affects only the text logged by Compute_Caps, not the lookup's result.
/// Whether BFME 2's retail string table has the same shape is not verified
/// here.
///
/// Provenance: the switch is Zero Hour's, case for case (compared).
/// Open-BFME-1's source has the same one.
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_get_ati_device Get_ATI_Device
/// <tt>static DeviceTypeATI DX8Caps::Get_ATI_Device(unsigned device_id)</tt>
/// maps an ATI PCI device identifier to a device enumerator, from the Rage II
/// to the Radeon 8500. Compute_Caps calls it when the vendor is ATI. Like the
/// 3dfx, Matrox and S3 lookups (and unlike the NVIDIA, 3Dlabs, Intel and
/// PowerVR ones), its result drives several rules in Vendor_Specific_Hacks
/// (Rage Pro, Rage 128 and Radeon workarounds).
///
/// A switch grouping identifiers into families: Rage II, II+ and IIC (PCI and
/// AGP), Rage Pro and Rage Pro Mobility, Rage 128 in its GL, VR, Pro GL,
/// Pro VR, Pro Ultra, 4X and Mobility M3 and M4 forms, Radeon VE, Radeon DDR,
/// Radeon 7500 and its mobile form, Mobility Radeon VE (M6), and the
/// Radeon 8500. Anything else returns the unknown ATI device.
///
/// Three enumerators are never returned by this function: the plain Rage 128
/// Mobility, the Mobility Radeon and the Radeon. The comparisons against
/// them in Vendor_Specific_Hacks never match. Provenance: Zero Hour's switch,
/// case for case (compared).
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_get_3dlabs_device Get_3DLabs_Device
/// <tt>static DeviceType3DLabs DX8Caps::Get_3DLabs_Device(unsigned device_id)</tt>
/// maps a 3Dlabs PCI device identifier to a device enumerator (the Permedia
/// line and related parts). Compute_Caps calls it for the 3Dlabs vendor and
/// logs the name. No workaround or driver rule depends on the result. A switch
/// over sixteen identifiers: thirteen of the numbers from 1 to 14 (5 is
/// absent) cover the 300SX, 500TX, Delta, Permedia, MX, Permedia2, Gamma,
/// Permedia2S (ST), Permedia3, R3, Permedia4, R4 and G2 parts, with Oxygen VX1,
/// TI P1 and a second Permedia2 identifier beside them. Anything else returns
/// the unknown device. Provenance: Zero Hour's switch, case for case
/// (compared).
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_get_3dfx_device Get_3Dfx_Device
/// <tt>static DeviceType3Dfx DX8Caps::Get_3Dfx_Device(unsigned device_id)</tt>
/// maps a 3dfx PCI device identifier to a device enumerator (Voodoo Graphics,
/// Voodoo 2, Banshee, Voodoo 3, Voodoo 5 and Voodoo Rush). Compute_Caps calls
/// it for the 3dfx vendor. The result feeds Vendor_Specific_Hacks (Voodoo2,
/// Banshee and Voodoo3 rules), and ShaderClass::Apply tests for the Voodoo3
/// device explicitly. A switch over seven identifiers. The Voodoo Rush device
/// has two of them: the source labels them an Alliance AT25 or AT3D based
/// reference board and a Macronix based one. Anything else returns the
/// unknown device. Provenance: Zero Hour's switch, case for case (compared).
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_get_matrox_device Get_Matrox_Device
/// <tt>static DeviceTypeMatrox DX8Caps::Get_Matrox_Device(unsigned device_id)</tt>
/// maps a Matrox PCI device identifier to a device enumerator (Millennium,
/// Mystique, G100, G200, G400, G550 and Parhelia). Compute_Caps calls it for
/// the Matrox vendor. Only the G400 and G550 results matter to
/// Vendor_Specific_Hacks. A switch over twelve identifiers, with separate PCI
/// and AGP entries for the G100, G200 and Millennium II, and one for each of
/// the others, including the Parhelia and its AGP 8X form. Anything else
/// returns the unknown device. Provenance: Zero Hour's switch, case for case
/// (compared).
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_get_powervr_device Get_PowerVR_Device
/// <tt>static DeviceTypePowerVR DX8Caps::Get_PowerVR_Device(unsigned device_id)</tt>
/// maps a PowerVR PCI device identifier to a device enumerator; only the Kyro
/// is listed. Compute_Caps calls it for the PowerVR vendor, to log the name.
/// The PowerVR workarounds in Vendor_Specific_Hacks test the vendor only, so
/// they apply to a PowerVR device that this function reports as unknown too.
/// One case: a single identifier returns the Kyro; everything else returns the
/// unknown device. Provenance: Zero Hour's body (compared).
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_get_s3_device Get_S3_Device
/// <tt>static DeviceTypeS3 DX8Caps::Get_S3_Device(unsigned device_id)</tt>
/// maps an S3 PCI device identifier to a device enumerator (Savage MX, Savage
/// 4 and the Savage 200). Compute_Caps calls it for the S3 vendor. The Savage 4
/// and Savage 200 results each trigger a rule in Vendor_Specific_Hacks. A
/// switch over three identifiers; anything else returns the unknown device.
/// Provenance: Zero Hour's switch (compared).
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_get_intel_device Get_Intel_Device
/// <tt>static DeviceTypeIntel DX8Caps::Get_Intel_Device(unsigned device_id)</tt>
/// maps an Intel PCI device identifier to a device enumerator; only the i810,
/// i810E and i815 are listed. Compute_Caps calls it for the Intel vendor, to
/// log the name. No workaround or driver rule is keyed on Intel hardware, so
/// every Intel adapter, including a current one that falls through to the
/// unknown device, passes through unchanged. A switch over three identifiers;
/// anything else returns the unknown device. Provenance: Zero Hour's switch
/// (compared).
///
/// \section grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_probes Capability probes
/// The probes are private members that Compute_Caps runs in a fixed order,
/// after the flags it reads straight from the caps record. Each writes either
/// one of the support tables or a flag, and most append a line to the full
/// text log.
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_init_caps Init_Caps
/// <tt>void DX8Caps::Init_Caps(IDirect3DDevice8* D3DDevice)</tt> reads the
/// device's capability record and sets the hardware transform-and-lighting
/// flag. It does one GetDeviceCaps query into the caps record, then sets the
/// flag from the hardware-transform-and-light bit of the record's device caps.
/// The query goes through the wrapper's own device (the DX8 call helper that
/// counts Direct3D calls), so the D3DDevice parameter is not used.
///
/// Zero Hour: the device constructor calls Init_Caps before Compute_Caps, and
/// Compute_Caps contains the call only as a commented-out line. Zero Hour's
/// version first turns on software vertex processing, reads the caps, and,
/// when the card has hardware transform and lighting, switches the device back
/// to hardware processing and reads the caps again. BFME 2's version does
/// neither. BFME 2's constructors perform the same two steps in their own
/// bodies and do not call Init_Caps, and its Compute_Caps does not call it
/// either; use tools/callers_of.py to see whether any caller of the function
/// is known. Open-BFME-1's source for this function keeps Zero Hour's
/// two-pass shape (using Direct3D 9's own software-vertex-processing method);
/// its match to the BFME 1 executable was not checked here. Provenance: the
/// single-query shape is read from the BFME 2 body; the two-pass shape is
/// Zero Hour's.
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_texture_format Check_Texture_Format_Support
/// <tt>void DX8Caps::Check_Texture_Format_Support(WW3DFormat display_format,
/// const D3DCAPS8& caps)</tt> fills the texture-format table: for each Direct3D
/// format code, whether the adapter can create a texture in that format given
/// the current display format. It is the first probe Compute_Caps runs. The
/// table is read through the format-support accessor, for example by
/// W3DRadar::initializeTextureFormats, which passes literal Direct3D codes
/// (20, 22, 23 and 24 for the terrain image) where Zero Hour's version names
/// its WW3D formats.
/// - An unknown display format (the value 0) clears the table and returns
///   without asking Direct3D anything. Device enumeration relies on that.
/// - Otherwise it calls CheckDeviceFormat once per table entry, with the
///   adapter ordinal and device type from the caps record, the display
///   format as the adapter format, no usage flags and the texture resource
///   type, and stores whether the call succeeded.
/// - The table index is the Direct3D format code itself for the ordinary
///   codes, which stay below 118 in the Direct3D 9 SDK headers of the period
///   (an inference from those headers, not from BFME 2 evidence). The last
///   five entries are for the DXT codes. They are queried with consecutive
///   integers starting at the DXT1 code, so the second to fifth entries ask
///   about the values one to four above the DXT1 code and not about the DXT2
///   to DXT5 FourCC codes. Open-BFME-1's source builds the same consecutive
///   series for its own shorter table. Whether the mapping is intentional is
///   not established. The rest of the unit treats the five entries as DXT1 to
///   DXT5 (see \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_compression and
///   \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules).
/// - No log line is written.
///
/// Zero Hour: the loop is over WW3DFormat, converts each entry with
/// WW3DFormat_To_D3DFormat and logs every supported format. The Direct3D
/// code indexing and the consecutive DXT series are read from the BFME 2 body.
///
/// Remastering: format codes are Direct3D 9 numbers on both sides of this
/// function, so a port needs a conversion in the accessors and in readers that
/// pass literal codes. The DXT entries need care: if a driver answers success
/// only for the real FourCC codes, the four entries after DXT1 read false.
/// That is a hazard to check, not a measurement.
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_render_target Check_Render_To_Texture_Support
/// <tt>void DX8Caps::Check_Render_To_Texture_Support(WW3DFormat display_format,
/// const D3DCAPS8& caps)</tt> fills the render-target table: for each Direct3D
/// format code, whether a texture in that format can be a render target.
/// Compute_Caps runs it after the texture-format probe. The table answers the
/// render-to-texture accessor, which DX8Wrapper's render target creation
/// checks before it creates a target. The shape is the same as the
/// texture-format probe, with the render-target usage flag and with entries for
/// the ordinary format codes only (there are no DXT slots). An unknown display
/// format clears the table without probing. No log line is written.
/// Vendor_Specific_Hacks can clear the whole table afterwards for specific
/// old cards (Rage Pro, Rage 128 Pro GL, the Radeon family named there, and
/// Voodoo3). Provenance: Zero Hour's structure with Direct3D 9 indexing
/// (compared).
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_depth_stencil Check_Depth_Stencil_Support
/// <tt>void DX8Caps::Check_Depth_Stencil_Support(WW3DFormat display_format,
/// const D3DCAPS8& caps)</tt> fills the depth-stencil table for the Direct3D
/// depth formats the game uses. Compute_Caps runs it after the render-target
/// probe. The accessor over this table is checked together with the
/// render-target one when a render target is created with its own depth buffer
/// (Zero Hour: DX8Wrapper::Create_Render_Target).
///
/// An unknown display format clears the table and returns. Otherwise the loop
/// runs over the codes 70 to 80, and for each code a switch selects the depth
/// formats: 70, 71, 73, 75, 77, 79 and 80 (D3DFMT_D16_LOCKABLE, D32, D15S1,
/// D24S8, D24X8, D24X4S4 and D16 in the Direct3D 9 header). For those it calls
/// CheckDeviceFormat with the depth-stencil usage flag and the surface resource
/// type, storing whether it succeeded. The other codes in the range are
/// skipped and keep whatever they held.
///
/// The switch also lists 82 and 83 (D3DFMT_D32F_LOCKABLE and D3DFMT_D24FS8,
/// the Direct3D 9 float depth formats), but the loop stops at 80, so they are
/// never probed. Entries other than the seven probed ones are not written by
/// this function at all, apart from the clear for an unknown display format.
/// Whether the object's memory starts zeroed was not checked, so treat the
/// other entries as undefined.
///
/// Zero Hour: loops over its WW3DZFormat values and asks for a texture
/// resource. The fixed list of seven codes and the surface resource type are
/// read from the BFME 2 body. Remastering: a renderer that wants float or
/// other depth formats must extend the loop and the table; both the list and
/// the table are keyed on Direct3D 9 codes.
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_compression Check_Texture_Compression_Support
/// <tt>void DX8Caps::Check_Texture_Compression_Support(const D3DCAPS8& caps)</tt>
/// sets the DXT compression flag if any of the five DXT format entries of the
/// texture table is set. Compute_Caps runs it right after the three format
/// probes, so the table is already filled. It ORs the five DXT entries (the
/// entries that the texture-format probe fills from the series that starts at
/// the DXT1 code), stores the result as the flag and appends "Texture
/// compression support: Yes" or "No" to the log. The caps parameter is not
/// used. On NVIDIA hardware, Vendor_Specific_Hacks recomputes the flag
/// afterwards without the DXT1 entry.
///
/// Zero Hour reads the flag in textureloader.cpp and ww3dformat.cpp (see Who
/// reads it above). Zero Hour's body ORs the same five entries and logs the
/// same line (compared); the BFME 2 body reads the entries by position in the
/// table.
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_bumpmap Check_Bumpmap_Support
/// <tt>void DX8Caps::Check_Bumpmap_Support(const D3DCAPS8& caps)</tt> sets the
/// environment-bump-mapping flags from the texture-op caps and logs them.
/// Compute_Caps runs it after the compression check. Two flags, each true when
/// the texture-op caps include the matching bit: environment bump mapping, and
/// environment bump mapping with luminance. Two log lines follow: "Bumpmap
/// support" and "Bumpmap luminance support", each Yes or No.
///
/// Readers. Zero Hour's texture initialisation (texture.cpp, Load_Texture) and
/// BFME 2's Load_Texture(ChunkLoadClass&) read the bump-envmap flag. When a W3D
/// texture is typed as a bump map and the flag is set, they pick the first
/// supported of U8V8, X8L8V8U8 and L6V5U5 from the texture-format table, after
/// forcing a single mip level. The luminance flag's only Zero Hour test is the
/// block in meshmatdesc.cpp that sits inside a comment (it would have
/// removed a render pass whose shader needs bump mapping on hardware that
/// lacks it); that block tests the plain flag as well. Whether BFME 2 reads the
/// luminance flag anywhere was not established here. Provenance: Zero Hour's
/// body (compared).
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_shader Check_Shader_Support
/// <tt>void DX8Caps::Check_Shader_Support(const D3DCAPS8& caps)</tt> records
/// the vertex and pixel shader versions and logs them. Compute_Caps runs it
/// after the bump-map check. The public accessors split each version into a
/// major and a minor number (the high and low bytes of the stored value). Zero
/// Hour: W3DShaderManager::getChipset uses the pixel shader version, with the
/// simultaneous-texture count, to class a card it does not recognise by vendor
/// and device.
///
/// It stores the vertex and pixel shader version words from the caps record as
/// they are, and appends the line "Vertex shader version: a.b, pixel shader
/// version: c.d", where a and b (and c and d) are the second and first bytes of
/// the stored version words. The stored word is Direct3D's own encoding, with
/// more in it than the two bytes this line prints: the enumeration code
/// compares the raw pixel shader word with the encoded version 1.1 value when
/// it rejects adapters (see DX8Wrapper::Enumerate_Devices). Provenance: Zero
/// Hour's body (compared).
///
/// \subsection grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_max_texture Check_Maximum_Texture_Support
/// <tt>void DX8Caps::Check_Maximum_Texture_Support(const D3DCAPS8& caps)</tt>
/// copies the hardware's simultaneous-texture count from the caps record into
/// the object. Compute_Caps runs it as the last probe. The stored value is the
/// raw hardware figure behind the accessor for the maximum number of
/// simultaneous textures (Zero Hour: W3DShaderManager::getChipset reads it). It
/// is separate from the textures-per-pass limit that Compute_Caps sets from the
/// same number and that Vendor_Specific_Hacks may then lower to one. The
/// per-pass limit is what the draw code loops over. The body is one store.
/// Provenance: Zero Hour's body (compared).
///
/// \section grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_driver_table Driver version table
/// <tt>void DX8Caps::Check_Driver_Version_Status()</tt> looks up the driver's
/// file name and build number in a table of drivers Westwood knew to be bad or
/// acceptable and records the verdict. Compute_Caps runs it after the vendor
/// and driver build have been stored. The verdict is an enumeration (unknown,
/// good, OK, bad) exposed through a public accessor. Zero Hour: nothing in its
/// code outside the class calls that accessor, so the verdict there reaches
/// only the log; use tools/callers_of.py and rg to see whether BFME 2 code
/// reads it.
///
/// The status starts as unknown. The vendor selects what is tested; the driver
/// file name is compared case-insensitively and the build number is the fourth
/// component of the driver version:
/// - 3dfx: every driver is bad.
/// - NVIDIA: nv4.dll with one build is bad. nv4_disp.dll and nvdd32.dll have a
///   table of builds: three are OK (2181, 2183 and 2240, with comments about
///   occasional lock-ups), thirteen are bad (twelve builds between 618 and
///   1440, and 2311), and any other build is bad if it is below 2000 and
///   unknown otherwise. Two Elsa DLLs (egdad.dll and egliid.dll) are tested
///   too.
/// - ATI: ati2dvag.dll has five builds (one unknown, one OK, three bad);
///   atid32ae.dll has one OK build and ati3drai.dll one unknown build.
/// - PowerVR: pmx2hal.dll always leaves the verdict at unknown.
/// - Everything else stays unknown.
///
/// The verdict is logged as one line, "Driver version status: Unknown", "OK (No
/// known problems)", "Good" or "Bad (Driver update recommended)".
///
/// \warning The Elsa tests are written with a default label before the case
/// label and no break, so execution falls through and the status ends as OK
/// for every build of those two DLLs, not only the build named in the case. The
/// status Good is never assigned anywhere in this function.
///
/// Remastering: the table lists driver DLL names and build numbers of its
/// period, and a driver with any other DLL name stays at unknown. A port can
/// drop the table or move it to data. Provenance: Zero Hour's logic (compared).
/// The driver names and the comments that name testers and their machines are
/// the source's own.
///
/// \section grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules Vendor-specific rules
/// <tt>void DX8Caps::Vendor_Specific_Hacks(const D3DADAPTER_IDENTIFIER8&
/// adapter_id)</tt> overrides capabilities and sets limits for specific vendors
/// and devices whose drivers misreport or mishandle them. It is the last step
/// of Compute_Caps. It patches the finished flags and tables, and each rule
/// writes a line to the caps log. The adapter parameter is not used; the rules
/// read the stored vendor and mapped device. The rules, by vendor:
/// - NVIDIA (any device): the N-patch flag is forced off (the source says the
///   driver reports it incorrectly) and the first DXT format entry is forced
///   off (the source says DXT1 is broken on NVIDIA hardware). Each is logged
///   first when it was set. The DXT compression flag is then recomputed from
///   the other four DXT entries.
/// - Matrox G400 and G550: the modulate-alpha-add-colour flag is cleared (it
///   cannot take a texture in the second argument).
/// - ATI Rage Pro and Rage Pro Mobility: one texture per pass, multipass off,
///   and the render-target table cleared.
/// - ATI Rage 128 Pro GL: multipass off and the render-target table cleared.
///   (The source comment mentions re-enabling multitexturing for a game; the
///   code does not touch the texture count here.)
/// - The ATI Rage 128 devices named in the code (the Mobility variants, Pro
///   Ultra, 4X, Pro GL, Pro VR, GL and VR): display ceiling 1280 by 1024 and
///   the modulate-alpha-add-colour flag cleared.
/// - ATI Mobility Radeon VE (M6), Radeon VE, Radeon DDR, Mobility R7500 and
///   R7500: the render-target table cleared. (The plain Mobility Radeon and
///   Radeon are in the test, but Get_ATI_Device never returns them.)
/// - ATI Radeon VE: anisotropic filtering off (the source cites a lock-up
///   report).
/// - 3dfx Voodoo2, Banshee and Voodoo3: one texture per pass and a 1280 by
///   1024 ceiling; Voodoo3 also loses its render-target table.
/// - PowerVR (any device): 1280 by 1024 ceiling and fog not allowed (the
///   source says scene-capture cards dislike fog colour changes mid-frame).
/// - S3 Savage 4: ceiling 800 by 600. The log line still says 1024 by 768, the
///   value left in a trailing comment next to the code.
/// - S3 Savage 200: one texture per pass and multipass off.
///
/// Intel and 3Dlabs adapters have no rules. The ceilings are stored where
/// DX8Caps::Is_Valid_Display_Format reads them; the flags are read through the
/// public accessors.
///
/// Zero Hour also has a GeForce2 MX rule, which the BFME 2 body does not have
/// (see Differences from Zero Hour above). Provenance: Zero Hour's rule list
/// (compared) with the log text unchanged. The BFME 2 differences are the
/// missing GeForce2 MX rule and the position-based access to the DXT entries
/// (Zero Hour's recompute includes the DXT1 entry it has just cleared, so the
/// result is the same). The reasons in the bullets are the source's comments,
/// not independent evidence about the drivers.
///
/// Modding: all of this is code. The effect a modder or player sees is on the
/// affected cards only: a capability switched off, or the enumeration leaving
/// out over-limit modes (see When the object is built). On those cards the
/// renderer sees one texture per pass, no render targets or no fog permission,
/// and its readers choose accordingly (for example ShaderClass::Apply and
/// Render2DClass::Render test the texture-per-pass limit).
///
/// Remastering:
/// - The rules for NVIDIA and PowerVR are by vendor, so they apply to every
///   device of that vendor, new or old. On NVIDIA hardware the DXT1 table
///   entry is off and the N-patch flag is off; the dynamic-texture exclusion
///   in Compute_Caps is also by vendor with a driver build test.
/// - The texture-per-pass override of one (Rage Pro, Voodoo2, Voodoo3,
///   Banshee, Savage 200) lowers what the draw code loops over.
/// - Stored limits that other code reads: the texture-per-pass limit, the
///   multipass flag and the fog flag. A port that drops these rules must keep
///   those fields valid.


/// \file WW3D2/dx8caps.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection
/// \brief Capability detection for the Direct3D adapter: vendor and device
///        lookup tables, the format probes, the driver-version table and
///        the vendor workarounds.
///
/// Westwood's WW3D2 unit, the same file as Zero Hour's dx8caps.cpp in
/// outline. Zero Hour's version also defines the log name tables for the
/// vendors and devices as file-scope arrays, indexed by the mapped device,
/// with a scratch string that formats each log line before it is appended.
/// The details of each function are in \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection.


/// \class DX8Caps
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection
/// \brief A snapshot of what one Direct3D adapter and driver can do, with
///        its vendor and device identity and the verdicts of the project's
///        workaround tables.
///
/// The renderer builds a DX8Caps once per adapter probe and once per device
/// and then only reads it. It holds the Direct3D capability record, a set of
/// yes-or-no flags (hardware transform and lighting, DXT compression,
/// gamma, N-patches, bump mapping, anisotropic filtering, cube maps, point
/// sprites, dynamic textures, dot-product blending and others), three
/// per-format support tables (texture, render target, depth-stencil), the
/// vertex and pixel shader versions, the number of textures a pass can
/// use, a maximum display size, the vendor, the mapped device and the
/// driver's file name, build number and verdict, and two text logs.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - DX8Wrapper owns the current instance, reached through
///   DX8Wrapper::Get_Current_Caps. DX8Wrapper::Do_Onetime_Device_Dependent_Inits
///   replaces it and DX8Wrapper::Do_Onetime_Device_Dependent_Shutdowns
///   deletes it. A device-enumeration probe object lives on the stack of
///   DX8Wrapper::Enumerate_Devices.
/// - There are two constructors: the device constructor queries
///   GetDeviceCaps on the wrapper's device and the caps-copy constructor
///   copies a caps record that the caller already holds (enumeration gets the
///   record from the Direct3D interface, not from a device). Both set the
///   hardware transform-and-lighting flag from the record, zero the
///   display-size limits and call Compute_Caps.
/// - The two logs and the driver file name are owned strings, released by the
///   class's out-of-line destructor.
/// - A file-scope scratch string used to format each log line is shared by
///   all instances, so two instances must not be built at the same time on
///   different threads. Zero Hour: DX8Caps::Shutdown, which DX8Wrapper's
///   shutdown calls, releases that scratch string. Whether BFME 2 has an
///   equivalent was not established.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// - Everything the probes and rules write is computed during construction:
///   the constructors fill the caps record and the transform-and-lighting
///   flag, then call DX8Caps::Compute_Caps, which runs every lookup, probe and
///   rule function described in the group. Init_Caps is the exception: it is
///   not on that path in BFME 2, because the constructors do its two steps
///   inline. Afterwards the object is read through the query accessors and, by
///   a few readers, through the raw caps record.
/// - The format tables are indexed by Direct3D format code. The texture table
///   has one entry per ordinary format code plus five more for the DXT codes,
///   the render-target table one entry per ordinary format code, and the
///   depth-stencil table the same, of which only seven are ever written (see
///   \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_depth_stencil). A probe with an unknown
///   display format has every written entry false.
/// - The mapped device index is only meaningful together with the vendor:
///   each vendor has its own device enumeration, and the index selects the
///   name in that vendor's name table. A separate field keeps the raw PCI
///   device identifier.
/// - The class has no virtual functions.
///
/// \par Private members
/// The vendor and device lookups, the capability probes, the driver-version
/// table and the vendor rules are private members, described on the group page
/// rather than here: \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_lookups,
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_probes,
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_driver_table and
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules.
///
/// \par Retail versus Zero Hour
/// BFME 2's object is larger and arranged differently from Zero Hour's: its
/// caps record is the Direct3D 9 one, it keeps the raw device identifier and a
/// dynamic-texture flag, and its format tables are longer. See the group page
/// for the full list. The state of the reconstruction of this class is not
/// described here; use the project's progress tools.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// See \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection for the Direct3D 9 specific parts
/// and the vendor and driver-build assumptions.
///
/// \see DX8Caps::Compute_Caps, \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules,
///      \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_driver_table, \ref sub_w3d_rendering


/// \fn void DX8Caps::Compute_Caps(WW3DFormat display_format, const D3DADAPTER_IDENTIFIER8& adapter_id)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection
/// \brief Fills in the whole capability record from the adapter identifier
///        and the already-loaded Direct3D caps: identity, flags, format
///        tables, shader versions, driver verdict and workarounds.
///
/// The one function that does the work; both constructors end with a call
/// to it. It runs the lookups and probes in a fixed order and builds the two
/// text logs as it goes. The full log gets one line per decision, each ending
/// in a carriage return and line feed; the compact log is a single
/// tab-separated line.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// In order:
/// -# Defaults and logs: multipass and fog are allowed; both logs are
///    cleared.
/// -# Identity: logs the card description and the driver file name; keeps
///    the driver file name; splits the driver version into product, version,
///    sub-version and build (two 16-bit halves of each of its two words) and
///    keeps only the build number, which later rules compare.
/// -# Vendor: Define_Vendor. When it returns the unknown vendor and the driver
///    file name begins with the character 3, the vendor is set to 3dfx, on the
///    source's reasoning that an unidentified driver with such a name can
///    hardly be anything else. The vendor name goes to the compact log; the raw
///    PCI device identifier is stored.
/// -# Device: a switch on the vendor picks the vendor's lookup
///    (Get_NVidia_Device, Get_ATI_Device, Get_Intel_Device, Get_S3_Device,
///    Get_PowerVR_Device, Get_Matrox_Device, Get_3Dfx_Device or
///    Get_3DLabs_Device); the result is stored as the mapped device and its
///    name goes to both logs. An unknown vendor stores 0. The compact log now
///    holds the vendor, the device name and the build number, separated by
///    tabs, as one line.
/// -# More identity in the full log: the vendor, device and subsystem
///    identifiers, the revision and the device GUID.
/// -# Flags straight from the caps record: point sprites (maximum point size
///    above 1), N-patches, depth bias, full-screen gamma, the
///    modulate-alpha-add-colour texture op, the dot-product-3 texture op,
///    cube maps, anisotropic filtering (both magnification and minification
///    bits) and dynamic textures (a Direct3D 9 caps bit). The dynamic-texture
///    flag is then cleared on NVIDIA hardware whose driver build is below
///    6176; the source's comment says older NVIDIA drivers falsely advertise
///    it. The log gets one line for each flag except cube maps, point sprites
///    and dynamic textures; the hardware transform-and-lighting line reports
///    the flag that the constructor set.
/// -# Probes, in this order: Check_Texture_Format_Support,
///    Check_Render_To_Texture_Support, Check_Depth_Stencil_Support,
///    Check_Texture_Compression_Support, Check_Bumpmap_Support,
///    Check_Shader_Support, Check_Driver_Version_Status and
///    Check_Maximum_Texture_Support. The compression check reads the
///    texture-format table, and the driver check reads the vendor and build
///    number stored earlier.
/// -# Textures per pass is taken from the caps record's simultaneous-texture
///    count and logged.
/// -# Vendor_Specific_Hacks runs last, so that its overrides patch finished
///    tables and flags. Finally the scratch string is emptied.
///
/// The probes and rules named above are described under
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_lookups,
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_probes,
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_driver_table and
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing here reads configuration. The detection leaves a text record in
/// the two logs. Zero Hour exposes them through public accessors that nothing
/// in its code outside the class calls; use rg and tools/callers_of.py to see
/// whether any BFME 2 code reads them. A modder who wants to see what the game
/// decided for a given card can inspect the object in a debugger or
/// reconstruct the lines from the inputs.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The adapter identifier that retail passes is Direct3D 9's layout (it has
///   a device-name string before the driver version). A port must supply the
///   fields this function reads: driver file name, description, driver
///   version, and the vendor, device, subsystem and revision identifiers and
///   the device GUID.
/// - This is the function to replace when porting; its inputs are the
///   vendor, device and driver identity, the caps record and the display
///   format, and its outputs are listed under DX8Caps.
/// - The driver build number is the last 16-bit part of the driver's
///   version, not a release number. Current drivers follow different
///   numbering, so build-number thresholds (the dynamic-texture exclusion
///   here, the table in \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_driver_table) do
///   not mean what they meant in 2002.
///
/// \param display_format The display format code that the format probes use
///        as the adapter format. The unknown value (0) makes the three
///        format probes clear their tables instead of querying.
/// \param adapter_id The adapter identifier in its Direct3D 9 layout. Read
///        only.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The skeleton and the log text are Zero Hour's. The Direct3D 9 layout of
/// the identifier, the raw device field, the dynamic-texture flag and the
/// NVIDIA build test are read from the BFME 2 body. The reason given for the
/// build limit comes from a comment in the reconstructed source; neither Zero
/// Hour's nor Open-BFME-1's source has this rule, and the reason was not
/// checked against anything else.
///
/// \see DX8Caps, \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules


/// \fn bool DX8Caps::Is_Valid_Display_Format(int width, int height, WW3DFormat format)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection
/// \brief Says whether a display mode of this size is allowed on this
///        adapter.
///
/// DX8Wrapper::Enumerate_Devices calls it for every display mode of the five
/// formats it enumerates; modes that fail are left out of the device's
/// resolution list, and the device is left out of the device list when none
/// passes. In the sources read for this page that enumeration is the caller of
/// this function, and so the point where the ceilings set by
/// \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules take effect. Zero Hour: the
/// options menu lists the resulting set through the getDisplayModeCount method
/// of W3DDisplay; BFME 2's consumer of the list was not verified.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// If both maximum dimensions are zero (no limit, the constructor's
/// default), every mode is valid. Otherwise a mode is valid when its width
/// is at most the maximum width and its height is at most the maximum
/// height. The format argument is not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The limits are not data. A card without a rule has no ceiling, so on it
/// the enumeration keeps every mode the driver reports for the five
/// enumerated formats.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Resolution is not otherwise limited here, so a port that wants to cap
/// or hide modes can add the test in this function, or at the caller.
///
/// \param width  Mode width in pixels.
/// \param height Mode height in pixels.
/// \param format Display format of the mode. Not used.
/// \return True if the mode fits within the limits, or there are none.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Zero Hour's body (compared). In BFME 2 the caller passes the Direct3D
/// format code, which this function ignores.
///
/// \see DX8Caps, \ref grp_w1023b_dx8_caps_detection_vendor_rules
